{"api_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/iree-org/iree/issues/16608","collected_at":"2026-09-27T21:55:58.810225+00:00","comments":[{"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","body":"@benvanik, The first report about this line, https://github.com/openxla/iree/blob/76cbaaca2cf44ac0e4f58cbce4134c253e38f758/runtime/src/iree/io/memory_stream.c#L267\r\n\r\nAnd the code path for loading an unaligned uint16 goes here:\r\nhttps://github.com/openxla/iree/blob/76cbaaca2cf44ac0e4f58cbce4134c253e38f758/runtime/src/iree/base/alignment.h#L364-L366\r\n\r\nIt is always undefined behavior to have a C or C++ object at a memory location whose address isn't a multiple of its alignment, so the UBSan report seems to be correct. No platform-specific trait can change that. Instead, platform-specific traits such as `__ARM_FEATURE_UNALIGNED` only merely mean an existence claim, that there is *some* way to perform an unaligned access. They don't suddenly change the C/C++ semantics to allow unaligned objects. In the case of `__ARM_FEATURE_UNALIGNED`, the Arm reference https://developer.arm.com/documentation/dui0774/g/chr1383660321827 explains __ARM_FEATURE_UNALIGNED as \"Set to 1 if the target supports unaligned access in hardware\".  This could mean a number of things (I don't know exactly in this case), such as possibly meaning that some builtin or intrinsic is available to perform that, I dont know --- but `alignment.h` in its current form is undefined behavior and the easy way out for now would be to just build with `IREE_MEMORY_ACCESS_ALIGNMENT_REQUIRED_*`.","created_at":"2024-02-28T21:07:49Z","html_url":"https://github.com/iree-org/iree/issues/16608#issuecomment-1969918686","id":1969918686,"issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/iree-org/iree/issues/16608","minimized":null,"node_id":"IC_kwDODGgK6M51apLe","performed_via_github_app":null,"pin":null,"reactions":{"+1":0,"-1":0,"confused":0,"eyes":0,"heart":0,"hooray":0,"laugh":0,"rocket":0,"total_count":0,"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/iree-org/iree/issues/comments/1969918686/reactions"},"updated_at":"2024-02-28T21:08:08Z","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/iree-org/iree/issues/comments/1969918686","user":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/79535?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/bjacob","id":79535,"login":"bjacob","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc5NTM1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/bjacob","user_view_type":"public"}},{"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","body":"Platform I ran on is x86_64.\r\n\r\nSo when using the unaligned functions, it's behaving as intended and the illegal instruction error I got from the test can be safely ignored (thanks)\r\n\r\nWhat about the error on https://github.com/openxla/iree/blob/main/runtime/src/iree/io/formats/gguf/gguf_parser.c#L417?\r\n\r\n[ RUN ] GgufFormatTest.SingleTensor\r\n/opt/work/iree/github-head/openxla/iree/runtime/src/iree/io/formats/gguf/gguf_parser.c:417:22: runtime error: load of misaligned address 0x558d6855052e for type 'const uint64_t' (aka 'const unsigned long'), which requires 8 byte alignment\r\n0x558d6855052e: note: pointer points here\r\n02 00 00 00 02 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 02 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00","created_at":"2024-02-28T21:19:44Z","html_url":"https://github.com/iree-org/iree/issues/16608#issuecomment-1969939235","id":1969939235,"issue_ur